  "read": {
    "headline": "The war premium leaks out of crude while the curve keeps flattening.",
    "standfirst": "Friday closed mixed — the S&P 500 added 0.2%, the Nasdaq 0.4%, the Dow lost 0.2% — and the ten-year settled at 5.01%, its first close above 5% since July 2007. Over the weekend crude kept falling: WTI is down 7.87% over five sessions and Brent traded under $102 this morning, the lowest since 10 September, on hopes of US–Iran contact at the UN General Assembly and Saudi pipeline repairs. The one-year column is blank across most of the board today because the source table does not carry it; currencies and crypto are the exceptions.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "The curve has given up half its slope in a month.",
        "text": "The two-year yield is 4.76% against 4.24% a month ago, a rise of 12.26%, while the thirty-year is 5.34% against 5.27%, a rise of 1.33%. That leaves 2s10s at 25bp, down from roughly 50bp a month ago. A curve flattening while the whole of it moves up is a market pricing a hiking cycle it expects to bite, not a market pricing a recession — 3M10Y is still 87bp positive and widened this week as the ten-year rose faster than the bill."
      },
      {
        "title": "Crude fell eight percent in five sessions. Energy equities did not.",
        "text": "WTI is down 7.87% over five days, gasoline 7.94% and heating oil 7.90%, as Saudi repair work and the prospect of US–Iran contact at the UN drained part of the war premium. Over the same five days the energy sector ETF is up 0.44%. Equity energy is still priced off a 43.84% year, not off last week's tape. One of the two is early."
      },
      {
        "title": "Gold is the odd asset out of an inflation shock.",
        "text": "Crude is up 63.64% year to date and wheat 54.74%, yet gold is up 0.24% and silver down 8.31%. Copper, the industrial metal, is up 26.61% over six months against gold's 1.00%. The monetary hedge has lost to the physical inputs all year, which is what a real-yield shock looks like rather than a currency-debasement one."
      },
      {
        "title": "Taiwan and Korea are the same headline and different trades.",
        "text": "Korea is up 86.49% year to date and Taiwan 75.73%, the two largest moves on the board. But Korea is down 17.22% over three months and up 1.67% over one, while Taiwan is flat over three months and up 7.04% over one. The semiconductor bid is still running in Taipei and stopped running in Seoul in June."
      },
      {
        "title": "Crypto is having a week inside a losing year.",
        "text": "Cardano is up 18.69% over five days, Chainlink 14.42%, Solana 13.71% and Ethereum 10.63%. Every one of them is still negative year to date except Chainlink at 0.27%, and Solana is down 52.57% over twelve months. A five-day squeeze inside a twelve-month drawdown is a different thing from a trend, and the size of the five-day numbers is the tell."
      },
      {
        "title": "Friday's selling was European and small, not American and broad.",
        "text": "Italy fell 1.85% on the day, Spain 1.72%, France 1.58%, the UK 1.42% and Germany 1.26%, against the US ETF's 0.12%. Inside the US the split was the same shape: technology rose 0.82% and industrials 0.44% while materials and utilities each fell 1.42%. On the factor grid, momentum beat the index by 1.26% and small-cap growth lagged by 0.32%. Breadth, not direction, was the story."
      }
    ]
  },
  "curve": {
    "title": "The US Treasury curve",
    "note": "Today against a month ago. The front end has done almost all of the moving: the two-year is up 12.26% over the month and the thirty-year 1.33%. The ten-year closed Friday at 5.01% after touching about 5.04% on 15–16 September, the highest since 2007. The rise is a real-yield rise — ten-year breakevens have moved less than 10bp since the war began against roughly 100bp on real yields.",
